%T A396696 37020015557311,4479421882434643,645036751070588593,
%U A396696 109011210930929472311,21366197342462176573007,4807394402053989728926577,1230692966925821370605203741,355670267441562376104903881179,115237166651066209857988857502039
%N A396696 a(n) is the smallest prime greater than n^2*a(n-1), with a(1) = 2.
%C A396696 Motivated by the following conjecture (see Guedes and Torres link): Is there a real number C so that [C*(n!)^2] is always a prime number for any positive integer?
%C A396696 Indeed, supposing that for every n>1 we have a(n) < n^2 * (a(n-1)+1), then there would be a sequence of nested intervals of the form (a(n)/((n!)^2), (a(n)+1)/((n!)^2)). Any real number C contained in the intersection of these intervals would satisfy the inequality a(n) < ((n!)^2)*C < (a(n)+1), or [C*(n!)^2] = a(n).
%C A396696 a(254) has 1005 digits. - _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 22 2026
%H A396696 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A396696/b396696.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..253</a>
%H A396696 Eric Campos Bastos Guedes and Anderson Torres, <a href="https://mail-archive.com/obm-l@mat.puc-rio.br/msg58136.html">Fórmula para Primos</a>.
%e A396696 a(2) = 11 since the smallest prime greater than 2^2*a(1) = 8 is 11.
%e A396696 a(3) = 101 since the smallest prime greater than 3^2*a(2) = 99 is 101.
%e A396696 a(4) = 1619 since the smallest prime greater than 4^2*101 = 1616 is 1619.
%o A396696 (PARI) lista(nn) = my(va= vector(nn)); va[1] = 2; for (n=2, nn, va[n] = nextprime(va[n-1]*n^2);); va; \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 21 2026
%o A396696 (PARI) a(n)=if(n==1, 2, nextprime(n^2*a(n-1))) \\ _Anderson Torres_, Aug 22 2026
%o A396696 (Python)
%o A396696 from itertools import count, islice
%o A396696 from gmpy2 import next_prime
%o A396696 def agen(): # generator of terms
%o A396696     an = 2
%o A396696     for n in count(2):
%o A396696         yield int(an)
%o A396696         an = next_prime(n**2*an)
%o A396696 print(list(islice(agen(), 18))) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 22 2026
